Phase A – Movement (Day 1)
Steps 1 to 3 (already planned)
Mount Phaser, run, jump. You're doing these now.
Step 4 – Dash and the state machine
Goal: a dash that feels crisp, and a clean state machine so run, jump, dash and flat form don't turn into a tangle of if statements.
Test: one air dash per jump, refreshed on landing. Ground dash works. Cooldown is noticeable. Dash cancels gravity for its duration.

Step 5 – Flat form and the slit
Goal: hold S to fold flat, slide through a low gap, and never get stuck.
Test: you can't walk through the slit standing. Flat, you can. Letting go of S inside the slit keeps you flat until there's room. Sliding in at full speed feels like a slide.

Step 6 – Level data and the movement lane
Goal: the level comes from a data file, not from code, and the movement lane exists with the dash gap and the slit.
Test: the 6-tile gap can't be crossed with a jump alone but can with a jump plus dash. The pit floor is safe and you can jump out of it.

Step 7 – Game feel: visuals and camera
Test: landing and dashing feel punchy, the camera never jerks, and nothing shakes when it shouldn't.

Step 8 – Sound
Test: each sound plays once per action, none of them is loud or harsh, and M mutes everything.

Step 9 – Debug overlay, tuning panel and reset

Phase B – Puzzle framework and the fold room 
Step 10 – Entities, rooms and reset
Goal: a reusable base for every puzzle object, plus rooms that save and restore their state.
Test: Backspace puts everything back exactly as it started. Dying or resetting never leaves a room half-changed.


Step 11 – Switch, fold platform and room 1A
Goal: the first real puzzle, with clear feedback.
Test: you can't reach the ledge without the switch. The stairs unfold one after another, collision turns on as each lands, and the dotted link line makes it obvious what the switch controls.

Step 12 – Tutorial prompts and hints
Goal: the level teaches without text walls.
Test: each prompt appears only near the spot where it applies, fades after you do the action once, and comes back with a soft pulse if you fail the same spot 3 times.


Phase C – Form and wind rooms, end of level 

Step 13 – Room 1B: the slit puzzle
Test: only flat form passes it, there's no way to stand through or jump over it, and the optional second slit reinforces the idea.

Step 14 – Room 1C: fan and wind
Test: the high platform is only reachable by riding the draught, falling into the pit never traps you, and steering inside the column feels good.

Step 15 – Pickup, sword toggle, exit

Phase D – Menus
Build these after Level 1 works from start to finish.

Step 16 – Scene flow and main menu
Screens and flow: Boot, Main menu, Game, Pause overlay, Level complete. The game scene never knows about the menu, it just reports events (level complete, quit).
Main menu contents: title "Unfolded", Play, Room select, Controls, Sound on/off. Room select lists every room in the level data (Movement lane, 1A, 1B, 1C), so Levels 2 and 3 appear automatically later. Keyboard (arrows, Enter) and mouse both work.
Look: same paper style as the game: mint background, cream paper buttons with dark outline, one purple highlight on the selected item, a slow-drifting origami shape behind the title drawn in code, quick fade between scenes.
Test: you can start the game, jump to any room directly, come back to the menu, and start again without a page reload or leftover sounds.


Step 17 – Pause menu
Contents: Resume, Restart room, Room select, Controls, Sound on/off, Quit to main menu. Esc opens and closes it. The game freezes behind a soft darkened overlay and the player's input is blocked while it's open.
Test: pausing mid-dash or mid-jump and resuming doesn't break the state, the timers don't keep counting while paused, music and fan hum pause with it, and Restart room puts everything back.

